## Python Instruction
Write a python function to decode a string encoded with Run-Length Encoding (RLE). RLE is a form of lossless data compression in which runs of data (sequences in which the same data value occurs in many consecutive data elements) are stored as a single data value and count, rather than as the original run. For example, the string "aaabccddd" would become "3a2c3d". Your task is to write a function that takes an encoded string as input and returns the original, uncompressed string.

## Python Solution
```python
def decode_rle(encoded_str):
    decoded_str = ""
    i = 0
    while i < len(encoded_str):
        count = ""
        while i < len(encoded_str) and encoded_str[i].isdigit():
            count += encoded_str[i]
            i += 1
        decoded_str += int(count) * encoded_str[i]
        i += 1
    return decoded_str

# Test cases
assert decode_rle("4a2b1c3d") == "aaaabbcddd"
assert decode_rle("3c2z1x") == "ccczzx"
assert decode_rle("10a") == "aaaaaaaaaa"
```

